\begin{didyouknow}{Archimedes Sums Forever}
In the third century BC, Archimedes found the area of a parabolic segment by filling it with triangles. Each new layer of triangles had one quarter the area of the layer before, so he needed the sum $1+\tfrac14+\tfrac1{16}+\cdots$. He had no modern theory of limits, but a careful argument showed him that the total is exactly $\tfrac43$. It is one of the earliest infinite geometric series ever summed.
\end{didyouknow}
% DYK-END

